Let $v(x)=\ln(x)$, then $\ln\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)=v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$, or $(v\circ u)(x)$.

Therefore, the derivative of $\ln\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)$ can be found using **the chain rule**:

>$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{d}{dx}\ln\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)
\\\\\\\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[v\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]&&\gray{\text{Let }v(x)=\ln(x)}
\\\\\\\\\\
&=v'\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\text{The chain rule}}
\\\\\\\\\\
&=\dfrac{1}{u(x)}\cdot u'(x)&&\gray{\dfrac{d}{dx}\ln(x)=\dfrac{1}{x}}
\\\\\\\\
&=\dfrac{u'(x)}{u(x)}
\end{align}$
